Wait, I have to show you.
等等，我得给你看看。

797
00:32:56.470 --> 00:32:58.930
I was listening to your donut selection.
我刚才在听你选甜甜圈。

798
00:32:59.210 --> 00:33:02.890
But I have to show you the picture of the birdhouse mailbox that I want.
但我得给你看看我想要的那个鸟屋邮箱的照片。

799
00:33:03.010 --> 00:33:03.230
Okay?
好吗？

800
00:33:03.570 --> 00:33:03.830
Okay.
好。

801
00:33:04.190 --> 00:33:06.950
It's like a three-story birdhouse.
这就像一个三层楼的鸟屋。

802
00:33:08.470 --> 00:33:11.750
It looks like a combo mailbox and birdhouse.
它看起来像是邮箱和鸟屋的结合体。

803
00:33:11.950 --> 00:33:12.330
It is.
确实是的。

804
00:33:12.330 --> 00:33:14.810
If I were a bird, I wouldn't want to move into that.
如果我是只鸟，我才不想搬进那里面去。

805
00:33:14.970 --> 00:33:15.570
It feels...
感觉……

806
00:33:15.570 --> 00:33:16.210
I don't know.
我不知道。

807
00:33:16.670 --> 00:33:21.210
It feels like it's going to be like living above a busy store.
感觉就像住在热闹的店铺楼上一样。

808
00:33:21.730 --> 00:33:27.270
Also, though, will I just have, like, bird poop on my mail?
不过话说回来，我的信件上会不会全都是鸟粪？

809
00:33:27.530 --> 00:33:28.450
I don't know.
我不知道。

810
00:33:28.770 --> 00:33:32.950
I guess Jim walks out of his office and notices that there's a bunch of people missing.
我想象吉姆走出办公室，发现少了一大群人。

811
00:33:33.370 --> 00:33:34.310
Where are they?
他们去哪儿了？

812
00:33:34.510 --> 00:33:35.210
I don't know.
我不知道。

813
00:33:35.210 --> 00:33:37.470
Maybe I'll go see what Michael's up to.
也许我去看看迈克尔在搞什么鬼。

814
00:33:37.670 --> 00:33:40.710
And he's having a sneaky meeting.
然后他在开一场鬼鬼祟祟的会议。

815
00:33:41.670 --> 00:33:42.190
Yeah.
没错。

816
00:33:42.490 --> 00:33:48.090
He's got Oscar, Stanley, Creed, and Kelly all sitting in chairs across his desk.
他让奥斯卡、斯坦利、克里德和凯莉都坐在他办公桌对面的椅子上。

817
00:33:48.810 --> 00:33:52.870
Michael claims it's a product expansion focus group.
迈克尔声称这是一个产品拓展焦点小组。

818
00:33:53.210 --> 00:33:55.310
They all want to go back to their desks.
他们其实都想回到自己的办公桌前。

819
00:33:55.530 --> 00:33:58.090
And Jim says, what kind of products?
然后吉姆问，什么样类型的产品？

820
00:33:58.110 --> 00:33:58.990
What happened in here?
这里刚才发生什么了？

821
00:33:59.210 --> 00:34:00.790
You know, what was the last thing that was said?
你知道的，刚才说的最后一句话是什么？

822
00:34:00.930 --> 00:34:04.790
And Stanley said, Michael said, if you don't smell this, you're fired.
然后斯坦利说，迈克尔说，如果你不闻闻这个，你就被解雇了。

823
00:34:08.810 --> 00:34:13.050
This leads to a Kelly talking head where she says she loves rivalries.
这引发了凯莉的一个单独访谈，她说她喜欢看竞争。

824
00:34:13.470 --> 00:34:16.090
Michael or Jim, Paris or Nicole, Heidi or Elsie.
迈克尔还是吉姆，帕丽斯还是妮可，海蒂还是埃尔茜。

825
00:34:16.250 --> 00:34:16.810
It's so fun.
太有趣了。

826
00:34:17.450 --> 00:34:21.130
This was the beginning, Jenna, of people comparing Jim and Michael as bosses.
詹娜，这就是人们开始把吉姆和迈克尔作为老板进行对比的开端。

827
00:34:21.610 --> 00:34:23.590
There are a series of deleted talking heads.
有一系列被删减的单独访谈。

828
00:34:23.730 --> 00:34:24.650
They are so fun.
它们太有趣了。

829
00:34:24.810 --> 00:34:25.590
We have to hear them.
我们必须听听看。

830
00:34:25.590 --> 00:34:26.870
Let's start with Toby's.
让我们从托比的开始。

831
00:34:28.030 --> 00:34:31.130
Comparing Jim and Michael is like comparing apples and oranges.
拿吉姆和迈克尔作比较，就像是在拿苹果和橙子作比较。

832
00:34:32.630 --> 00:34:34.070
I like apples.
我喜欢苹果。

833
00:34:34.230 --> 00:34:35.630
I really, really don't like oranges.
我真的、真的很不喜欢橙子。

834
00:34:36.590 --> 00:34:38.830
Oranges are annoying and hate me.
橙子很烦人，而且讨厌我。

835
00:34:43.750 --> 00:34:45.890
Oranges are annoying and hate me.
橙子很烦人，而且讨厌我。

836
00:34:46.310 --> 00:34:46.690
Hmm.
嗯。

837
00:34:46.890 --> 00:34:47.730
Who's the orange?
谁是那个橙子？

838
00:34:47.930 --> 00:34:48.870
That made me laugh.
这逗笑了我。

839
00:34:49.210 --> 00:34:53.270
Now for maybe my favorite Stanley talking head ever.
接下来也许是我有史以来最喜欢的斯坦利单独访谈。

840
00:34:53.270 --> 00:34:55.030
And it wasn't in the episode.
而它并没有出现在正片里。

841
00:34:55.030 --> 00:34:56.030
And I'm so sad.
我太伤心了。

842
00:34:56.170 --> 00:34:56.950
You have to hear it.
你们一定要听听。

843
00:34:58.620 --> 00:35:01.740
When you ask Jim a question, he'll tell you an answer.
当你问吉姆一个问题时，他会告诉你一个答案。

844
00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:06.540
When you ask Michael a question, he'll do an imitation of you for five minutes.
当你问迈克尔一个问题时，他会模仿你五分钟。

845
00:35:06.820 --> 00:35:09.860
Then he'll show you three YouTube videos.
然后他会给你看三个YouTube视频。

846
00:35:10.020 --> 00:35:11.780
Then he'll try to tickle you.
接着他会试图胳肢你。

847
00:35:11.920 --> 00:35:15.180
And if that's not enough, he'll ask you to repeat the question.
如果这还不够，他会让你重复那个问题。

848
00:35:15.980 --> 00:35:18.160
Meanwhile, he's wandered off somewhere.
与此同时，他已经溜达到别的地方去了。

849
00:35:18.340 --> 00:35:21.840
He comes back, looks at the clock, and leaves for the rest of the day.
等他回来，看了一眼时钟，然后就离开直到下班。

850
00:35:22.420 --> 00:35:25.460
But he also doesn't care if you come in late.
但他也不介意你迟到。

851
00:35:25.680 --> 00:35:27.020
So I prefer Michael.
所以我更喜欢迈克尔。

852
00:35:28.420 --> 00:35:29.440
That is amazing.
这太神奇了。

853
00:35:29.820 --> 00:35:32.580
I love that he said first he'll do an impression of you.
我太喜欢他说他会先模仿你。

854
00:35:32.660 --> 00:35:34.260
Then he'll show you YouTube videos.
然后他会给你看YouTube视频。

855
00:35:35.520 --> 00:35:36.920
It just cracked me up.
我直接笑裂了。

856
00:35:37.420 --> 00:35:40.880
Lastly, you have to hear Andy's comparison of Jim and Michael.
最后，你们必须听听安迪对吉姆和迈克尔的对比。

857
00:35:41.200 --> 00:35:45.740
Because Andy is doing that thing, Jenna, that we do when a question makes us uncomfortable.
因为安迪在做那件事，珍娜，就是我们每当被问到一个尴尬问题时都会做的事。

858
00:35:47.180 --> 00:35:49.220
Michael is like the fun dad.
迈克尔就像有趣的爸爸。

859
00:35:49.520 --> 00:35:51.680
And Jim is like the serious mom.
而吉姆就像严厉的妈妈。

860
00:35:52.280 --> 00:35:54.740
And little Andy is stuck in the middle.
而小安迪被夹在中间。

861
00:35:54.940 --> 00:35:56.980
But he loves them both very much.
但他非常爱他们两个。

862
00:35:57.240 --> 00:36:01.000
But if he had to pick one, he would probably pick Jim.
但如果非要选一个，他可能会选吉姆。

863
00:36:01.220 --> 00:36:03.360
Because Jim gives him his num-nums on time.
因为吉姆总是按时给他好吃的。

864
00:36:04.400 --> 00:36:06.460
Num-nums are, of course, my commission checks.
好吃的，当然就是我的佣金支票。

865
00:36:08.620 --> 00:36:09.580
Oh, Andy.
哦，安迪。

866
00:36:10.080 --> 00:36:14.200
But we've talked about this, how sometimes you get asked a question that you're like,
但我们讨论过这个，有时候你被问到一个问题，你会觉得，

867
00:36:14.260 --> 00:36:17.000
I don't want to, this might come across mean to someone.
我不想回答，这可能会显得我对某人不太好。

868
00:36:17.040 --> 00:36:19.060
I'll do it in a little voice or I'll sing it.
我会用夹子音说或者唱出来。

869
00:36:20.320 --> 00:36:23.880
Yeah, the lady at the nursery was real mean to me.
对啊，花圃的那位女士对我可凶了。

870
00:36:24.140 --> 00:36:27.480
Yeah, she told me peace lilies have to stay inside.
对啊，她告诉我白掌必须放在室内。

871
00:36:27.480 --> 00:36:29.340
And I didn't like it one bit.
我一点都不喜欢这样。

872
00:36:35.180 --> 00:36:38.640
Well, now we're going to get into the major conflict of this episode.
好了，现在我们要进入这集的主要冲突了。

873
00:36:39.260 --> 00:36:44.940
David Wallace is going to call and explain that Dunder Mifflin cannot give out cost of living raises this year.
大卫·华莱士会打电话解释说邓德米夫林今年无法发放生活成本补贴。

874
00:36:45.680 --> 00:36:47.580
There's a finite amount of money.
资金是有限的。

875
00:36:47.660 --> 00:36:53.020
And it's up to these guys to figure out how to distribute the money amongst all the employees.
而他们几个得想办法把这笔钱分配给所有员工。

876
00:36:53.500 --> 00:36:55.640
And Michael and Jim start vying for this.
然后迈克尔和吉姆开始为此争夺。

877
00:36:55.780 --> 00:36:56.640
They're like, I got it.
他们俩就说，我来搞定。

878
00:36:56.720 --> 00:36:57.280
No, I got it.
不，我来搞定。

879
00:36:57.360 --> 00:36:58.780
And David's like, is there going to be a problem?
大卫就问，有什么问题吗？

880
00:36:58.960 --> 00:37:00.760
Also, Michael, are you texting me?
还有，迈克尔，你是在给我发短信吗？

881
00:37:01.720 --> 00:37:02.400
Oh, yeah.
哦，对。

882
00:37:03.100 --> 00:37:07.400
I guess in the end, David is like, you need to figure this out together.
我想最后大卫的意思是，你们需要一起把这事搞定。

883
00:37:08.140 --> 00:37:08.940
Terrible idea.
糟糕的主意。

884
00:37:09.760 --> 00:37:15.600
It starts some really funny Jim and Michael scenes because at first, Michael's acting like that.
这引发了一些非常搞笑的吉姆和迈克尔的对手戏，因为一开始，迈克尔表现得好像。

885
00:37:15.740 --> 00:37:16.280
Sure, great.
没问题，太棒了。

886
00:37:16.380 --> 00:37:17.160
They can do this together.
他们可以一起完成。

887
00:37:17.400 --> 00:37:19.060
And then Jim is like, tell me how you really feel.
然后吉姆就说，说出你的真实感受吧。

888
00:37:19.160 --> 00:37:21.700
And he goes, I hate the fact that we have to do this together.
接着他说，我讨厌我们必须一起做这事。

889
00:37:21.940 --> 00:37:26.000
And then he's like, all I can see is your big and gross pores on your nose.
然后他又说，我满眼看到的都是你鼻子上又大又恶心的毛孔。

890
00:37:26.000 --> 00:37:28.500
Like, it gets so personal.
真的，太人身攻击了。

891
00:37:29.180 --> 00:37:31.540
Later, he points out his crusty eyes.
后来，他还指出他眼角有眼屎。

892
00:37:31.720 --> 00:37:32.800
Like, what?
真是的，什么？

893
00:37:32.960 --> 00:37:35.720
Michael, what happens to you when you get back to the corner?
迈克尔，你一退到角落里就变成什么样了？

894
00:37:35.860 --> 00:37:37.160
You just get really mean.
你就是变得超级刻薄。

895
00:37:37.640 --> 00:37:42.880
Lady, I literally made a list of all of Michael's jabs at Jim.
天哪，我甚至列了一张表，记下了迈克尔对吉姆的所有挖苦。

896
00:37:43.400 --> 00:37:46.360
He also calls him Skippy at one point.
他甚至还在某个时候叫他“小鬼”。

897
00:37:46.600 --> 00:37:48.820
Big pores, crusty eyes, Skippy.
大毛孔、眼屎、小鬼。

898
00:37:49.640 --> 00:37:51.320
That was as far as my list went.
我的清单也就记到这儿了。

899
00:37:51.500 --> 00:37:51.600
Oh.
哦。

900
00:37:54.580 --> 00:37:59.020
Also, we learned that Michael hates a pros and cons liSt.
此外，我们了解到迈克尔讨厌利弊分析清单。

901
00:37:59.280 --> 00:38:00.340
Oh, my gosh.
噢，天哪。

902
00:38:01.060 --> 00:38:01.680
Hates it.
讨厌它。

903
00:38:02.200 --> 00:38:03.400
Absolutely hates it.
简直讨厌透了。

904
00:38:03.580 --> 00:38:08.360
And then he has that amazing quote where he says, sometimes the smartest people don't think at all.
然后他有一句绝妙的名言，他说，有时候最聪明的人反而什么都不想。

905
00:38:09.800 --> 00:38:12.160
Well, lady, I had to know.
好吧，女士，我非得知道不可。

906
00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:13.180
I don't know why.
我不知道为什么。

907
00:38:13.300 --> 00:38:19.060
I just on the fly decided to Google who invented the pros and cons liSt.
我刚才临时起意，用谷歌搜索了一下是谁发明了利弊清单。

908
00:38:19.300 --> 00:38:20.280
Did you really?
真的吗？

909
00:38:20.280 --> 00:38:22.540
The answer is fascinating.
答案太迷人了。

910
00:38:22.860 --> 00:38:23.700
Oh, my gosh.
噢，天哪。

911
00:38:23.780 --> 00:38:24.560
I want to hear it.
我想听听。

912
00:38:24.780 --> 00:38:29.920
There is an inventor of the pros and cons list, and it is Ben Franklin.
利弊清单确实有个发明者，那就是本·富兰克林。

913
00:38:30.660 --> 00:38:31.040
What?
什么？

914
00:38:31.460 --> 00:38:32.600
Ben Franklin.
本·富兰克林。

915
00:38:33.060 --> 00:38:33.700
All right.
好吧。

916
00:38:33.840 --> 00:38:36.900
This is where everyone thinks the pros and cons list came from.
这就是大家以为利弊清单的来源。

917
00:38:37.200 --> 00:38:45.940
He wrote a letter in 1772 to Joseph Priestley where he described in great detail how he makes a decision.
他在1772年给约瑟夫·普里斯特利写了一封信，在信中详细描述了他如何做决定。

918
00:38:46.620 --> 00:38:48.120
I'm going to read it to you.
我要读给你听。

919
00:38:48.380 --> 00:38:50.160
Every word fascinated me.
每一个字都让我着迷。

920
00:38:50.830 --> 00:38:57.460
He said, quote, my way, he capitalized way, by the way, his way.
他说，引述一下，“我的”方式，顺便说一句，他把“方式”首字母大写了，也就是他自己的方式。

921
00:38:57.720 --> 00:38:58.180
Okay.
好吧。

922
00:38:58.740 --> 00:39:00.520
Frank Sinatra, we hear you.
弗兰克·辛纳屈，我们听到你的心声了。

923
00:39:01.220 --> 00:39:10.680
Is to divide a half sheet of paper by a line into two columns, writing over the one, pro, and over the other, con.
“就是把一张纸对半画一条线分成两栏，在一栏上方写上‘利’，在另一栏上方写上‘弊’。”

924
00:39:10.680 --> 00:39:24.820
Then, during three or four days of consideration, I put down under the different headings different motives that at different times occur to me for or against the measure.
“然后，经过三四天的思考，我把在不同时间想到的、支持或反对某项措施的各种动因，分别写在不同的标题下。”

925
00:39:25.520 --> 00:39:37.720
When I have thus got them all together in one view, where I find two, one on each side that seem equal, I strike them both out.
“当我把它们全部汇集在一处一目了然时，如果我发现两边各有一个看似分量相当的理由，我就把它们两个都划掉。”

926
00:39:38.360 --> 00:39:44.640
If I find a reason, pro, that is equal to two reasons, con, I'll strike out all three.
“如果我发现一个‘利’的理由相当于两个‘弊’的理由，我就把这三个全划掉。”

927
00:39:45.520 --> 00:39:55.700
If I judge two reasons, con, equal to three reasons, pro, I strike out all five and thus proceed until the balance lies.
“如果我认为两个‘弊’的理由相当于三个‘利’的理由，我就把这五个全划掉，以此类推，直到权衡出结果。”

928
00:39:55.700 --> 00:40:05.340
And if, after a day or two of further consideration, nothing new of importance occurs on either side, I can come to a determination accordingly.
“而且，如果在经过一两天的进一步思考后，双方都没有出现什么新的重要情况，我就可以据此做出决定。”

929
00:40:06.060 --> 00:40:10.180
Then he said, I have found great advantage from this kind of equation.
然后他说，“我发现这种方程式给我带来了巨大的好处。”

930
00:40:10.500 --> 00:40:15.980
And he said, I believe I have discovered what could be called moral or prudential algebra.
他还说，“我相信我发现了可以称之为道德或谨慎代数的东西。”

931
00:40:16.520 --> 00:40:20.740
He believes that he had found a mathematical equation for making a decision.
他相信自己找到了一个做决定的数学方程式。

932
00:40:21.360 --> 00:40:25.120
Well, I can tell you right now, he would have driven Michael Scott crazy.
好吧，我现在就可以告诉你，他会把迈克尔·斯科特逼疯的。

933
00:40:25.560 --> 00:40:26.000
Crazy.
疯掉。

934
00:40:26.280 --> 00:40:29.920
If he walked in and said, listen, it's going to take me three days.
如果他走进来，然后说，听着，我要花三天时间。

935
00:40:30.120 --> 00:40:30.560
Yeah.
是的。

936
00:40:30.740 --> 00:40:33.840
With this pros and cons list to figure out what we're going to do.
用这个利弊清单来弄清楚我们该做什么。

937
00:40:33.840 --> 00:40:35.220
Michael would have been like, I'm out.
迈克尔肯定会说，我走了。

938
00:40:35.620 --> 00:40:40.760
Well, when I read this, I also realized I've been doing pros and cons lists wrong, I guess.
好吧，当我读到这段时，我也意识到，我以前做利弊清单的方法可能都做错了。

939
00:40:40.940 --> 00:40:46.140
I just make lists of pros and cons, and then I can see them all in one place.
我只是列出利与弊的清单，然后我就可以把它们全部看得很清楚。

940
00:40:46.540 --> 00:40:51.900
I didn't realize I was supposed to strike them out if I consider them to be of equal importance.
我以前不知道，如果我认为它们重要性相当，我是应该把它们划掉的。

941
00:40:52.560 --> 00:40:56.620
I didn't realize there was a whole striking out process that Ben Franklin intended.
我不知道本·富兰克林原本的意图是包含一整套划掉的过程。

942
00:40:57.360 --> 00:40:58.740
Well, Jenna, I know you love a liSt.
好吧，珍娜，我知道你超爱列清单。

943
00:40:58.960 --> 00:41:02.740
I have no doubt you've made many pro and con lists in your life.
毫无疑问，你这辈子做过很多利弊清单。

944
00:41:02.940 --> 00:41:03.480
True, true.
确实，确实。

945
00:41:03.840 --> 00:41:09.600
Well, I'll have you know, Angela, next Jim is going to make a pie chart of Michael's time.
好吧，安吉拉，我得告诉你，接下来吉姆要给迈克尔的时间做一个饼图。

946
00:41:10.180 --> 00:41:11.860
How Michael spends time.
迈克尔是如何度过时间的。

947
00:41:12.240 --> 00:41:12.440
Yeah.
是的。

948
00:41:12.660 --> 00:41:15.000
It looks like he used crayon, by the way.
顺便说一句，看起来他是用水彩笔画的。

949
00:41:15.280 --> 00:41:15.980
I know.
我知道。

950
00:41:16.300 --> 00:41:18.620
I also Googled who invented the pie chart.
我也用谷歌搜索了是谁发明的饼图。

951
00:41:18.940 --> 00:41:19.900
Did you really?
真的吗？

952
00:41:20.340 --> 00:41:20.760
Yeah.
对啊。

953
00:41:21.300 --> 00:41:22.280
Who did it?
谁干的？

954
00:41:22.620 --> 00:41:23.200
Oh, lady.
哦，天哪。

955
00:41:23.860 --> 00:41:25.020
Also fascinating.
而且还挺有意思的。

956
00:41:25.200 --> 00:41:28.060
Who knew these Googles would be so interesting?
谁知道这些谷歌搜索会这么有趣？

957
00:41:28.060 --> 00:41:34.720
The earliest known pie chart is generally credited to William Playfair in 1801.
最早的有记录的饼图通常被认为是由威廉·普莱费尔于1801年发明的。

958
00:41:34.720 --> 00:41:39.740
He was a businessman, engineer, and economics writer from Scotland.
他是一位来自苏格兰的商人、工程师和经济学作家。

959
00:41:40.900 --> 00:41:42.460
But get this.
但你绝对想不到。

960
00:41:42.680 --> 00:41:43.060
What?
什么？

961
00:41:43.660 --> 00:41:50.220
Florence Nightingale is credited with adapting the pie chart and basically making it go viral.
据说弗洛伦斯·南丁格尔改进了饼图，并基本上让它火遍了大江南北。

962
00:41:50.220 --> 00:41:52.140
Did she use it for pain?
她是用它来统计疼痛程度吗？

963
00:41:52.320 --> 00:41:53.960
Like, what's your threshold for pain?
比如，你的痛感阈值是多少？

964
00:41:54.420 --> 00:41:54.760
No.
不是。

965
00:41:54.980 --> 00:42:01.700
But it's interesting you ask that because I will remind people that Florence Nightingale is the woman who pioneered the field of nursing.
不过你这么问挺有意思的，因为我要提醒大家，南丁格尔女士正是开创护理学领域的先驱。

966
00:42:01.700 --> 00:42:03.380
She started a nursing school.
她创办了一所护士学校。

967
00:42:04.160 --> 00:42:09.180
She tended to wounded soldiers during the Crimean War.
她在克里米亚战争期间照顾受伤的士兵。

968
00:42:09.980 --> 00:42:19.620
But she was also a pioneer in the area of statistics, and she made a pie chart that showed the impact of poor sanitary conditions on mortality rates.
但她也是统计学领域的先驱，她制作了一个饼图，展示了糟糕的卫生条件对死亡率的影响。

969
00:42:20.420 --> 00:42:26.500
And some people believe that this is the first time that a pie chart was used to persuade people to change.
有些人认为，这是历史上第一次使用饼图来劝说人们做出改变。

970
00:42:27.000 --> 00:42:28.000
Oh, wow.
哦，哇。

971
00:42:28.500 --> 00:42:30.700
And it kind of went viral.
而且它差不多就这么火了。

972
00:42:30.700 --> 00:42:35.820
So, she's credited as, you know, making the pie chart accessible.
所以，人们认为她让饼图变得通俗易懂。

973
00:42:36.620 --> 00:42:37.440
There you go.
就是这样。

974
00:42:37.760 --> 00:42:38.540
There you go.
就是这样。

975
00:42:38.660 --> 00:42:43.040
Well, I looked at Jim's pie chart, took a picture of it, and you know what I thought?
我看了吉姆的饼图，拍了张照片，你猜我怎么想的？

976
00:42:43.580 --> 00:42:44.080
What?
什么？

977
00:42:44.300 --> 00:42:45.860
He was just procrastinating.
他只是在拖延时间。

978
00:42:47.360 --> 00:42:50.660
I mean, really, how different would Jim's pie chart be from Michael's?
我是说，真的，吉姆的饼图能和迈克尔的有什麽不同？

979
00:42:50.860 --> 00:42:52.840
The biggest part is in green.
最大的一块是绿色的。

980
00:42:52.840 --> 00:42:54.180
It's procrastinating.
那就是拖延。

981
00:42:54.480 --> 00:42:58.640
Then the other biggest part is yellow, and it's distracting others.
然后第二大块是黄色的，是干扰他人。

982
00:42:58.640 --> 00:43:02.120
And then there's a tiny sliver of red for critical thinking.
接着有一小条红色的，代表批判性思维。

983
00:43:02.880 --> 00:43:04.720
I mean, Jim's wouldn't be that different.
我是说，吉姆的图也不会有什么两样。

984
00:43:04.940 --> 00:43:06.120
He's always distracted.
他总是心不在焉。

985
00:43:06.120 --> 00:43:07.440
He's procrastinating.
他在拖延。

986
00:43:07.720 --> 00:43:08.180
Come on.
拜托。

987
00:43:08.640 --> 00:43:09.940
You're so right.
你太对了。

988
00:43:10.280 --> 00:43:12.880
Jim just drew his own pie chart.
吉姆刚刚自己画了个饼图。

989
00:43:13.020 --> 00:43:13.760
Pretty much.
差不多吧。

990
00:43:14.220 --> 00:43:20.340
While Jim's making pie charts, Pam is still trying to hustle up some money as wedding gifts.
就在吉姆制作饼图的同时，帕姆还在努力拉扯着一些婚礼红包钱。

991
00:43:20.340 --> 00:43:23.400
And finally, find someone who's going to write her a check.
总算找到一个愿意给她开支票的人了。

992
00:43:23.920 --> 00:43:24.440
Kevin.
凯文。

993
00:43:24.960 --> 00:43:27.320
He writes her a check for $40.
他开了一张40美元的支票给她。

994
00:43:28.160 --> 00:43:33.360
And he says he's going to put to love's eternal glory in the subject line.
他说他要在备注栏写上“献给永恒的爱”。

995
00:43:33.640 --> 00:43:36.520
But also, you know, can you wait until you cash it till Monday?
但同时，你知道的，能等到周一再去兑现吗？

996
00:43:37.280 --> 00:43:44.920
Yes, but this is the moment that Pam sees the name Mrs. Pam Halpert for the first time.
是的，但就在这一刻，帕姆第一次看到了“帕姆·哈尔珀特太太”这个名字。

997
00:43:45.440 --> 00:43:45.720
Aw.
噢。

998
00:43:46.180 --> 00:43:47.500
She gets all excited.
她兴奋得不得了。

999
00:43:47.780 --> 00:43:49.120
And I have a question.
我有个问题。

1000
00:43:49.480 --> 00:43:54.360
Pam is doing her talking head out in the little, I don't know, what would you call that?
帕姆在那个小单采间里接受采访，我不知道，那叫什么来着？

1001
00:43:54.480 --> 00:43:55.880
The vestibule?
门厅？

1002
00:43:56.180 --> 00:44:01.500
The, like, inner lobby from right outside the door of Dunder Mifflin?
就，邓德米夫林门外那个内部大堂？

1003
00:44:01.780 --> 00:44:02.400
Oh, yeah.
哦，对。

1004
00:44:02.500 --> 00:44:03.440
Like the entryway.
就像入口那样。

1005
00:44:03.440 --> 00:44:09.460
Yeah, that Dunder Mifflin sign that Pam is standing next to, isn't that the one that
对，帕姆站着的那个邓德米夫林标志，不就是那个

1006
00:44:09.460 --> 00:44:12.680
John, like, took and you saw him putting it in his trunk?
约翰，就像是拿走了，你看到他把它放进后备箱了吗？

1007
00:44:12.920 --> 00:44:13.220
Yeah.
对。

1008
00:44:13.400 --> 00:44:13.940
At the end.
在最后。

1009
00:44:14.220 --> 00:44:16.340
That's the one John stole when we wrapped.
那是我们杀青时约翰偷走的那个。

1010
00:44:16.760 --> 00:44:17.760
That's what I thought.
我也这么想。

1011
00:44:17.960 --> 00:44:20.200
I was like, oh, hey, John's got that at his house.
我当时就想，哦，嘿，约翰家里有那个。

1012
00:44:20.400 --> 00:44:20.600
Yeah.
对。

1013
00:44:22.060 --> 00:44:26.480
Well, listen, I think we should take a break because when we come back, Jim and Michael
听着，我觉得我们该休息一下，因为等我们回来，吉姆和迈克尔

1014
00:44:26.480 --> 00:44:31.480
are going to try a lot of strategies to divide up this money.
会尝试各种策略来分配这笔钱。

1015
00:44:31.480 --> 00:44:33.860
And pretty much none of them are going to go well.
而且几乎没有一个会顺利的。

1016
00:44:34.380 --> 00:44:40.340
You mean putting beans on people's faces isn't a great way to figure out if they get a raise?
你是说在别人脸上放豆子不是弄清他们该不该加薪的好办法？

1017
00:44:41.160 --> 00:44:43.100
We'll talk about it after the break.
我们休息过后聊这个。

1018
00:44:43.420 --> 00:44:43.900
We will.
会的。

1019
00:44:54.180 --> 00:44:56.040
Jim and Michael are in the conference room.
吉姆和迈克尔在会议室里。

1020
00:44:56.560 --> 00:45:00.920
And Jim proposes that they give the raises to the salespeople.
吉姆提议把加薪给销售人员。

1021
00:45:01.540 --> 00:45:06.140
He said they're the ones who bring in the money and this will keep them happy.
他说他们才是赚钱的人，这能让他们保持高兴。

1022
00:45:06.920 --> 00:45:09.700
Well, Michael doesn't think that's going to go over very well.
好吧，迈克尔觉得这不会太受欢迎。

1023
00:45:10.440 --> 00:45:12.740
Well, these guys are going to kind of argue now.
好吧，这些人现在要开始争论了。

1024
00:45:12.900 --> 00:45:15.960
And I thought this scene was a little bit dramatic.
我觉得这场戏有点戏剧性。

1025
00:45:16.280 --> 00:45:17.980
And even Jen Salata mentioned it.
甚至珍·萨拉塔也提到了这一点。

1026
00:45:18.260 --> 00:45:19.480
Here's what she had to say.
她是怎么说的。

1027
00:45:20.280 --> 00:45:23.880
When I was directing a scene with Steve and John in the conference room, I think it was
当我执导史蒂夫和约翰在会议室的那场戏时，我想那是

1028
00:45:23.880 --> 00:45:27.500
the scene where Michael criticizes Jim's way of thinking and Jim tells Michael that he
迈克尔批评吉姆的想法，而吉姆告诉迈克尔他

1029
00:45:27.500 --> 00:45:27.980
has weaknesses.
有弱点的戏。

1030
00:45:28.380 --> 00:45:32.020
I kind of popped out of the moment and looked at us and said, I think we're doing a drama
我从那个情境中抽离出来，看着我们说，我觉得我们现在在演一部

1031
00:45:32.020 --> 00:45:32.520
right now.
正剧。

1032
00:45:32.920 --> 00:45:36.760
It occurred to me that I wrote it kind of dramatic and their instincts were dramatic as
我突然意识到我写得有点戏剧化，他们的直觉也很戏剧化，

1033
00:45:36.760 --> 00:45:37.860
was fitting for the scene.
正如这场戏所需要的。

1034
00:45:37.980 --> 00:45:40.400
And I was steering it in an even more dramatic way.
而且我把它引导向了一个更戏剧化的方向。

1035
00:45:40.400 --> 00:45:44.820
I remember trying to push things to their edges a bit more in the sixth season, maybe as a
我记得在第六季时曾试图把事情推向更边缘一点，也许是因为

1036
00:45:44.820 --> 00:45:48.680
reaction to having done so many episodes in a row fifth season that I was looking to shake
在第五季连续拍了那么多集之后的反应，我当时想打破

1037
00:45:48.680 --> 00:45:49.280
things up a little.
常规。

1038
00:45:49.480 --> 00:45:51.880
And drama is definitely where some of those scenes went.
而正剧确实是其中一些戏份的走向。

1039
00:45:52.200 --> 00:45:55.020
Maybe with me writing and directing, there wasn't enough quality control.
也许由于我既是编剧又是导演，质量把关还不够。

1040
00:45:55.220 --> 00:45:57.500
I think I even asked Paul, like, is this too dramatic?
我想我甚至问过保罗，这会不会太戏剧化了？

1041
00:45:57.740 --> 00:45:58.740
Did I make this into a drama?
我把它变成了一部正剧吗？

1042
00:45:59.260 --> 00:46:04.180
But then John and Steve and I paused and talked about the balance and landed in a really
但接着约翰、史蒂夫和我停下来讨论了平衡，最后达到了一个

1043
00:46:04.180 --> 00:46:05.060
nice place, I think.
非常棒的平衡点，我想。

1044
00:46:05.060 --> 00:46:08.400
Like, I love what Steve and John did in that scene and throughout that episode.
比如，我喜欢史蒂夫和约翰在那场戏以及整集里的表现。

1045
00:46:08.580 --> 00:46:12.500
It was really fun getting to dig into the relationship in a deeper way.
能以更深的方式深入探讨这种关系真的很有趣。

1046
00:46:13.600 --> 00:46:15.240
Yeah, I can feel that in this scene.
是的，在这场戏里我能感受到这一点。

1047
00:46:15.400 --> 00:46:17.940
I mean, Jim's like, you know what, Michael, here are your weaknesses.
我是说，吉姆就像是，你知道吗，迈克尔，你的弱点是这些。

1048
00:46:18.560 --> 00:46:20.740
And Michael's like, oh, yeah, you want to tell me my weaknesses?
而迈克尔就像是，哦，是吗，你想告诉我我的弱点？

1049
00:46:20.920 --> 00:46:22.180
Like, it gets really personal.
这变得真的很私人。

1050
00:46:22.380 --> 00:46:25.280
Not to mention Michael goes there with the crusty eyes.
更不用说迈克尔还提到了眼屎多那事。

1051
00:46:25.880 --> 00:46:26.160
Yes.
对。

1052
00:46:27.360 --> 00:46:33.420
I really like it when our show would dare to kind of let comedy go for a second.
我真的很喜欢我们的剧敢于在某些时候暂时放下喜剧效果。

1053
00:46:33.900 --> 00:46:36.020
Sometimes it was in those romantic moments.
有时是在那些浪漫的时刻。

1054
00:46:36.220 --> 00:46:41.500
Sometimes it was emotional with all the stuff about sprinkles, all the stuff about your heart
有时是充满情感的，比如所有关于斯普林克尔的事，所有关于你心碎的事，

1055
00:46:41.500 --> 00:46:42.580
getting broken, Angela.
安吉拉。

1056
00:46:42.900 --> 00:46:48.320
Like, I liked it when the show didn't need to be funny or ring that funny bell every three
比如，我喜欢这部剧不需要每隔三秒就刻意搞笑或去敲响搞笑的警钟，

1057
00:46:48.320 --> 00:46:50.900
seconds, which is kind of your traditional comedy.
那种有点像传统情景喜剧的做法。

1058
00:46:51.100 --> 00:46:52.940
So I really appreciated this scene.
所以我真的很欣赏这场戏。

1059
00:46:53.160 --> 00:46:54.080
I did, too.
我也是。

1060
00:46:54.300 --> 00:46:57.360
And of course, they found, like, the moments of comedy.
而且当然，他们也找到了那些喜剧效果的瞬间。

1061
00:46:57.560 --> 00:47:01.780
And one of the lines that made me laugh was at the very end when, you know, Jim is going
让我笑出声的其中一句台词是最后，你知道的，吉姆准备

1062
00:47:01.780 --> 00:47:04.260
to go out there, Michael's like, OK, Skippy, give it your best shot.
出去，迈克尔说，好吧，小家伙，拿出你的最佳水平。

1063
00:47:04.380 --> 00:47:04.900
Go tell him.
去告诉他吧。

1064
00:47:05.500 --> 00:47:06.500
And Jim leaves.
然后吉姆离开了。

1065
00:47:06.640 --> 00:47:09.960
And then Michael turns to Cameron and says, this had better be terrible.
接着迈克尔转向卡梅伦说，这最好结果很糟糕。

1066
00:47:10.420 --> 00:47:10.700
Yeah.
对。

1067
00:47:11.400 --> 00:47:13.480
He's rooting for him to fail.
他在盼着他失败呢。

1068
00:47:14.040 --> 00:47:17.820
And when Jim does just lay it out there, he's honeSt.
而当吉姆真的把情况全盘托出时，他是诚实的。

1069
00:47:17.980 --> 00:47:19.480
He's going to shoot everyone straight.
他打算对每个人都直言不讳。

1070
00:47:19.700 --> 00:47:21.260
And right away, it doesn't go well.
结果马上就不顺利了。

1071
00:47:21.740 --> 00:47:23.540
Well, he comes off condescending.
嗯，他显得高高在上。

1072
00:47:23.820 --> 00:47:28.900
He's talking about, I'm going to talk to you all like adults because you're very smart.
他说着，我要像对成年人一样跟你们谈，因为你们很聪明。

1073
00:47:29.340 --> 00:47:29.700
Yes.
对。

1074
00:47:30.040 --> 00:47:33.120
And then Oscar says, thank you, Jim, for thinking that we're smart people.
然后奥斯卡说，谢谢你，吉姆，觉得我们是聪明人。

1075
00:47:33.400 --> 00:47:37.540
And then Angela, sort of playing dumb, goes, but I do not understand.
接着安吉拉装傻似地说道，但我实在不明白。

1076
00:47:37.660 --> 00:47:40.320
If we're such smart adults, then why don't we get raises?
如果我们是这么聪明的成年人，那为什么我们没有加薪？

1077
00:47:40.900 --> 00:47:43.060
And Oscar's like, excellent question, genius.
然后奥斯卡说，问得好啊，天才。

1078
00:47:43.580 --> 00:47:47.800
Also, by the way, if you go back and rewatch this, tell me if you think I sound Southern
另外，顺便说一句，如果你回去重看这一段，告诉我当你听到我说“我不明白”时，

1079
00:47:47.800 --> 00:47:51.740
when I say, I do not understand, because it's all I could hear was my accent.
觉得我听起来像南方人吗，因为我满耳听到的都是自己的口音。

1080
00:47:52.400 --> 00:47:55.260
But anyway, so the scene starts to really get out of hand.
不过总之，这场戏开始变得彻底失控了。

1081
00:47:55.380 --> 00:47:56.920
And it builds with this crescendo.
并且情绪层层递进，达到了高潮。

1082
00:47:57.040 --> 00:47:58.100
You've got to hear it.
你们得听听这个。

1083
00:47:58.100 --> 00:48:00.460
We work just as hard as the salespeople.
我们的工作和销售人员一样努力。

1084
00:48:00.600 --> 00:48:01.420
We deserve raises.
我们理应加薪。

1085
00:48:01.560 --> 00:48:01.760
Yes.
没错。

1086
00:48:01.940 --> 00:48:02.160
Yes.
对。

1087
00:48:02.340 --> 00:48:03.960
They don't get them if we don't get them.
他们拿不到，我们就拿不到。

1088
00:48:04.100 --> 00:48:05.600
My kid needs shoes.
我的孩子需要鞋子穿。

1089
00:48:05.800 --> 00:48:07.820
You want to tell him he doesn't get shoes?
你想告诉他他没鞋穿吗？

1090
00:48:08.040 --> 00:48:08.240
Yeah.
对啊。

1091
00:48:08.380 --> 00:48:09.820
Her kid needs shoes, Jim.
她的孩子需要鞋子，吉姆。

1092
00:48:10.080 --> 00:48:11.060
What the hell?
搞什么鬼？

1093
00:48:15.220 --> 00:48:17.180
I mean, Michael got his wish.
我是说，迈克尔如愿以偿了。

1094
00:48:17.480 --> 00:48:18.440
That went terribly.
进展太糟糕了。

1095
00:48:19.100 --> 00:48:19.540
Terribly.
糟透了。

1096
00:48:20.020 --> 00:48:22.760
Jim is going to need to rewind out of that.
吉姆得赶紧把这一切倒回重来。

1097
00:48:22.760 --> 00:48:27.360
He's going to do his best little rewind back into the conference room.
他要使出浑身解数倒退回会议室里。

1098
00:48:27.480 --> 00:48:31.160
And Pam's face where she's like, oh, no, not rewind, guy.
还有帕姆的表情，仿佛在说，哦，不，不是倒回重来吧，老兄。

1099
00:48:31.340 --> 00:48:32.580
Not rewind, guy.
不是倒回重来吧，老兄。

1100
00:48:32.900 --> 00:48:36.520
I went to the script, and that reaction was in the script.
我查过剧本，那个反应是剧本里写好的。

1101
00:48:36.800 --> 00:48:41.360
It literally says, Pam is embarrassed for Jim.
上面字面写着，帕姆为吉姆感到尴尬。

1102
00:48:41.800 --> 00:48:46.880
Jenna, I don't know if you remember one time when I did a rewind bit, but it killed.
珍娜，我不知道你还记不记得有一次我做了一个倒带的梗，效果炸裂。

1103
00:48:46.880 --> 00:48:49.420
Do you remember you and I?
你还记得你和我吗？

1104
00:48:49.420 --> 00:48:50.240
Yes, I do.
记得，我当然记得。

1105
00:48:50.560 --> 00:48:57.020
We co-presented at the 57th American Cinema Editor's Awards, and Jenna and I announced
我们在第57届美国剪辑师协会奖上共同颁奖，我和珍娜宣布了

1106
00:48:57.020 --> 00:49:01.560
the winner of the best edited one-hour series for commercial television.
商业电视类最佳一小时剧集剪辑奖的得主。

1107
00:49:01.560 --> 00:49:03.400
I did my rewind bit.
我做了我的倒带梗。

1108
00:49:03.720 --> 00:49:04.660
Jenna, come on.
珍娜，拜托。

1109
00:49:04.820 --> 00:49:06.040
It was so good.
真的太棒了。

1110
00:49:06.200 --> 00:49:07.040
It was so good.
真的太棒了。

1111
00:49:07.120 --> 00:49:07.620
Want to hear it?
想听听吗？

1112
00:49:08.260 --> 00:49:09.720
First of all, we had great banter.
首先，我们的互动非常棒。

1113
00:49:09.840 --> 00:49:11.440
I thought we were great co-presenters.
我觉得我们是非常棒的搭档。

1114
00:49:11.880 --> 00:49:17.180
I thought we should get asked to host a whole award show based on that one appearance.
我觉得凭那一次亮相，我们就应该被邀请去主持整个颁奖典礼。

1115
00:49:17.240 --> 00:49:18.280
I really did.
我真的是这么想的。

1116
00:49:18.440 --> 00:49:22.560
I was so pleased with us that I was like, I'm sorry.
我对我们的表现太满意了，当时我就想，不好意思。

1117
00:49:22.960 --> 00:49:24.500
Give us a call, Emmys.
艾美奖，给我们打电话吧。

1118
00:49:24.500 --> 00:49:26.380
We need to host the whole shebang.
我们需要主持整场盛会。

1119
00:49:26.740 --> 00:49:28.760
Or at least present one award.
或者至少颁一个奖也行。

1120
00:49:29.640 --> 00:49:30.120
Yeah.
是啊。

1121
00:49:30.940 --> 00:49:32.160
Here's my editor's bit.
这就是我的剪辑师包袱。

1122
00:49:32.360 --> 00:49:32.520
Ready?
准备好了吗？

1123
00:49:32.840 --> 00:49:33.120
Yeah.
好。

1124
00:49:33.400 --> 00:49:34.300
So we did this bit.
所以我们演了这么个段子。

1125
00:49:34.400 --> 00:49:36.240
Jenna, remember you set up how we would walk by.
珍娜，还记得你设计的我们怎么走过去吗？

1126
00:49:36.240 --> 00:49:38.800
Yeah, I was like, we are very close with our editors.
对，我当时说，我们和剪辑师关系非常密切。

1127
00:49:38.800 --> 00:49:41.880
We're always walking by their edit bay, and here's what we hear.
我们总是路过他们的剪辑室，这是我们听到的声音。

1128
00:49:42.160 --> 00:49:43.620
And then I jumped in and went,
然后我插话道，

1129
00:49:49.600 --> 00:49:50.860
It's so good.
太棒了。

1130
00:49:51.040 --> 00:49:53.980
And I crushed it.
而且我表现得惊艳全场。

1131
00:49:56.440 --> 00:50:00.540
And you folks listening out there, guess who won the award we presented?
各位听众朋友们，猜猜我们颁发的那个奖是谁拿了？

1132
00:50:00.540 --> 00:50:05.020
It was none other than the editors for Friday Night Lights.
正是《胜利之光》的剪辑师们。

1133
00:50:05.500 --> 00:50:06.240
So cool.
太酷了。

1134
00:50:06.440 --> 00:50:07.040
So cool.
太酷了。

1135
00:50:07.140 --> 00:50:09.200
And it was a show Jenna and I both loved.
而且那是一部我和珍娜都很喜欢的剧。

1136
00:50:09.360 --> 00:50:10.720
We were so excited to meet them.
我们能见到他们超级兴奋。

1137
00:50:11.020 --> 00:50:12.340
That was a really fun night.
那真是一个有趣的夜晚。

1138
00:50:12.460 --> 00:50:14.080
I found fancy photos of us.
我找到了我们的精美照片。

1139
00:50:14.400 --> 00:50:16.980
Oh, I found fancy photos of us from that night.
哦，我找到了我们当晚的精美照片。

1140
00:50:17.140 --> 00:50:21.180
And then also, Jenna, I meant to bring up your red carpet photos for the promotion.
还有，珍娜，我本想提起你宣传时走红毯的照片。

1141
00:50:21.340 --> 00:50:24.320
You were all about the over the shoulder.
你一直在摆回眸的姿势。

1142
00:50:24.700 --> 00:50:25.660
I was.
我是摆了。

1143
00:50:25.660 --> 00:50:27.100
You're like, tonight's the night.
你当时就像是，今晚就是今晚。

1144
00:50:27.280 --> 00:50:30.380
I am going to finally get the over the shoulder look.
我终于要拍到回眸的造型了。

1145
00:50:30.660 --> 00:50:31.960
Wait, I have to show you.
等等，我得给你看看。

1146
00:50:32.680 --> 00:50:33.860
That's very funny.
太搞笑了吧。

1147
00:50:34.160 --> 00:50:38.940
You know, we did from time to time, a red carpet would come up and we'd be like, listen,
你知道的，我们有时要走红毯时就会想，听着，

1148
00:50:39.480 --> 00:50:42.200
I'm going to nail this new situation.
我要搞定这个新姿势。

1149
00:50:42.320 --> 00:50:43.440
I don't know what it would be.
我也不知道那会是什么。

1150
00:50:43.520 --> 00:50:47.720
We'd be like, I'm going to get the pout right tonight, or I'm going to nail an over the shoulder.
我们就会想，我今晚要把嘟嘴练好，或者我一定要摆好回眸造型。

1151
00:50:48.120 --> 00:50:50.920
I clearly decided to take on over the shoulder.
我明明决定要承担那个双肩拍摄。

1152
00:50:51.440 --> 00:50:53.440
The promotion was your night for it.
这次升职对你来说正是好时机。

1153
00:50:53.760 --> 00:50:55.380
I'll put it in stories, guys.
我会发到快拍里，伙计们。

1154
00:50:55.660 --> 00:50:56.420
I can't wait.
我等不及了。

1155
00:50:56.660 --> 00:51:00.220
Well, listen, in this whole scene, we did get a fan question from Heather B.
好吧，听着，在整场戏里，我们确实收到了来自海瑟·B的粉丝提问。

1156
00:51:00.440 --> 00:51:03.960
in Victoria, British Columbia, Canada, who noticed,
她来自加拿大不列颠哥伦比亚省维多利亚市，她注意到，

1157
00:51:04.240 --> 00:51:10.360
when Oscar tells The Office that Jim is only giving raises to his friends and the people he sleeps with,
当奥斯卡告诉《办公室》里的人说吉姆只给他的朋友和与之发生关系的人加薪时，

1158
00:51:10.780 --> 00:51:14.880
Pam turns around in her chair and mouths something in Oscar's direction.
帕姆在椅子上转过身去，朝着奥斯卡的方向无声地说着什么。

1159
00:51:15.160 --> 00:51:17.340
It's at 13 minutes, 18 seconds.
时间在13分18秒处。

1160
00:51:17.580 --> 00:51:19.120
Jenna, what were you saying?
珍娜，你刚才说什么？

1161
00:51:19.900 --> 00:51:20.940
Oh, my God.
噢，天哪。

1162
00:51:21.120 --> 00:51:22.220
I saw it.
我看到了。

1163
00:51:22.880 --> 00:51:26.880
I'm saying Oscar, but they cut my audio.
我说的是“奥斯卡”，但他们把我的音频剪掉了。

1164
00:51:27.520 --> 00:51:29.920
And I went to the script and it was not scripted.
我翻看了剧本，发现那不是剧本里写的。

1165
00:51:30.020 --> 00:51:32.180
So clearly I improvised Oscar.
所以我显然是即兴喊了奥斯卡的名字。

1166
00:51:32.500 --> 00:51:35.920
And when they got in the editing room, they were like, we are not interested in this improvisation.
当他们进入剪辑室时，他们大概觉得，我们对这段即兴发挥不感兴趣。

1167
00:51:36.140 --> 00:51:37.320
We are cutting your audio.
我们要剪掉你的音频。

1168
00:51:37.540 --> 00:51:38.600
We're cutting you out.
我们要把你剪掉。

1169
00:51:40.140 --> 00:51:47.260
So anyway, after Jim rewinds back into the conference room, they still have to make this decision.
言归正传，在吉姆倒回到会议室之后，他们仍然得做出这个决定。

1170
00:51:47.840 --> 00:51:51.580
Michael suggests maybe they do a merit-based raise.
迈克尔建议也许他们可以搞个按表现加薪。

1171
00:51:51.580 --> 00:51:58.780
And this is going to lead them to putting the Boston baked beans on the photos of the employees.
而这将引导他们在员工的照片上贴上波士顿烤豆糖。

1172
00:51:59.500 --> 00:52:05.080
But first, we're going to hear from the largest talking head in office history.
但首先，我们要听到《办公室》历史上人数最多的一段单人访谈。

1173
00:52:05.500 --> 00:52:06.540
Six people.
六个人。

1174
00:52:06.840 --> 00:52:09.060
It's at 14 minutes, 45 seconds.
时间在14分45秒处。

1175
00:52:09.320 --> 00:52:13.000
There is sort of a pyramid shape of office co-workers.
办公室同事们排成了一种金字塔形状。

1176
00:52:13.560 --> 00:52:14.400
Creed at the top.
克里德在顶端。

1177
00:52:14.540 --> 00:52:17.120
In the back row, Meredith, Kevin, and Angela.
后排是梅雷迪思、凯文和安吉拉。

1178
00:52:17.320 --> 00:52:19.100
In the front row, Dwight and Oscar.
前排是德怀特和奥斯卡。

1179
00:52:19.820 --> 00:52:21.640
And they are not happy.
他们很不高兴。

1180
00:52:22.000 --> 00:52:24.640
Meredith says, I am so pissed at this company.
梅雷迪思说，我对这家公司气死了。

1181
00:52:25.100 --> 00:52:25.460
Yeah.
是啊。

1182
00:52:25.900 --> 00:52:27.220
And then Dwight goes, and Jim.
然后德怀特说，还有吉姆。

1183
00:52:28.040 --> 00:52:29.460
And Meredith said, who says that?
梅雷迪思说，这话谁说的？

1184
00:52:29.700 --> 00:52:31.420
And Dwight says, I think it was Creed.
德怀特说，我想是克里德。

1185
00:52:31.540 --> 00:52:33.080
Creed's like, yep, Creed hadn't spoken.
克里德一脸“没错”，其实克里德之前根本没说话。

1186
00:52:33.340 --> 00:52:34.300
What's happening, Creed?
克里德，怎么回事？

1187
00:52:36.780 --> 00:52:38.680
I loved the big group talking head.
我很喜欢那个大型集体单人访谈。

1188
00:52:38.680 --> 00:52:39.940
I thought it was really fun.
我觉得真的很有意思。

1189
00:52:40.100 --> 00:52:40.600
I did too.
我也是。

1190
00:52:40.680 --> 00:52:44.580
It was fun to shoot as well because we each had a line and it was sort of like rapid fire.
拍摄起来也很有趣，因为我们每个人都有一句台词，有点像机关枪似的发问。

1191
00:52:44.800 --> 00:52:46.140
And it was just a ton of fun.
而且真的超级好玩。

1192
00:52:46.540 --> 00:52:50.240
Well, listen, Dwight is going to sneak into the conference room and he's going to discover
好吧，听着，德怀特会潜入会议室，然后他会发现

1193
00:52:50.740 --> 00:52:53.100
all these little beans on everybody's face.
每个人的脸上都贴着这些小豆子糖。

1194
00:52:53.100 --> 00:52:54.700
And he's going to blow their cover.
而他要揭穿他们的老底。

1195
00:52:54.960 --> 00:53:00.240
I did a deep dive on Boston baked beans because why not?
我对波士顿烤豆糖进行了深入研究，因为为什么不呢？

1196
00:53:00.460 --> 00:53:01.500
As you do.
就像你平时做的那样。

1197
00:53:01.820 --> 00:53:03.840
Here's what I'm going to let you know.
下面我要告诉你们这些。

1198
00:53:04.040 --> 00:53:07.020
Were they invented in the 14th century by?
它们是在14世纪由谁发明的吗？

1199
00:53:07.340 --> 00:53:08.080
I'm just kidding.
我开玩笑呢。

1200
00:53:08.480 --> 00:53:14.320
Well, first of all, I have to say, as I researched them, they sound delicious.
好吧，首先我得说，当我研究它们的时候，听起来还挺好吃的。

1201
00:53:14.320 --> 00:53:21.940
I've never really been drawn to the Boston baked bean because the name baked bean, it
我从来没有真正被波士顿烤豆吸引过，因为“烤豆”这个名字，它

1202
00:53:21.940 --> 00:53:25.240
doesn't like make me want to eat it as a candy.
听起来并不让我觉得想把它当成糖果吃。

1203
00:53:25.820 --> 00:53:27.420
So, I mean.
所以，我是说。

1204
00:53:27.660 --> 00:53:28.760
Wait, they're candy?
等等，它们是糖果？

1205
00:53:29.100 --> 00:53:30.060
They're candy.
它们是糖果。

1206
00:53:30.560 --> 00:53:31.380
See, right?
你看，对吧？

1207
00:53:31.740 --> 00:53:32.440
Oh, my God.
噢，天哪。

1208
00:53:32.440 --> 00:53:34.300
I thought they were putting actual beans.
我还以为它们放的是真正的豆子呢。

1209
00:53:35.020 --> 00:53:39.420
No, Boston baked beans are a sugar coated peanut candy.
不，波士顿烤豆是一种裹着糖衣的花生糖。

1210
00:53:39.700 --> 00:53:41.000
Oh, yes.
噢，是的。

1211
00:53:41.000 --> 00:53:41.480
Okay.
好的。

1212
00:53:41.860 --> 00:53:47.480
The name Boston baked bean is a generic name used throughout the whole candy industry to
“波士顿烤豆”这个名字是整个糖果行业通用的名称，

1213
00:53:47.480 --> 00:53:49.220
mean sugar coated peanut.
意思就是糖衣花生。

1214
00:53:49.960 --> 00:53:56.460
But the Ferrara Pan Candy Company developed their line of the Boston baked beans in the
但费拉拉潘糖果公司在

1215
00:53:56.460 --> 00:53:57.860
early 1930s.
20世纪30年代初开发了他们的波士顿烤豆系列。

1216
00:53:58.020 --> 00:54:01.040
Those are the ones in the red box that you see all the time.
就是你经常在红盒子里面看到的那种。

1217
00:54:01.360 --> 00:54:01.860
Right, right.
对的，对的。

1218
00:54:01.860 --> 00:54:06.920
They're made in this process called cold panned candy process.
它们是通过一种叫做冷式抛光糖果工艺的过程制作的。

1219
00:54:07.600 --> 00:54:12.360
And the pans kind of like move in a circle or something, almost like a record.
而且那些锅有点像是在转圈或者干嘛，几乎就像一张唱片。

1220
00:54:12.660 --> 00:54:19.480
And you throw the peanuts in and then like the sugar kind of coats them slowly until you
你把花生扔进去，然后糖慢慢地包裹着它们，直到

1221
00:54:19.480 --> 00:54:20.740
get them the right size.
你得到合适的大小。

1222
00:54:21.420 --> 00:54:22.540
I don't know.
我不知道。

1223
00:54:22.860 --> 00:54:24.600
They sound really good.
听起来真的很好吃。

1224
00:54:25.060 --> 00:54:27.180
Do you need some Boston baked beans, lady?
女士，你需要一些波士顿烤豆吗？

1225
00:54:27.300 --> 00:54:28.420
Do I need to drop some off?
我需要给你送一些过来吗？

1226
00:54:28.620 --> 00:54:29.380
Maybe I do.
也许我需要。

1227
00:54:29.580 --> 00:54:31.180
Why did I only get a plant?
为什么我只收到了一株植物？

1228
00:54:31.420 --> 00:54:34.020
Why didn't I also get Boston baked beans in a donut?
为什么我没有同时在甜甜圈里也得到波士顿烤豆？

1229
00:54:34.020 --> 00:54:34.940
I don't know.
我不知道。

1230
00:54:35.560 --> 00:54:41.580
I did find out a few fun facts about the Ferrara Company that makes Boston baked beans that
我确实发现了几个关于制造波士顿烤豆的费拉拉公司的趣事，

1231
00:54:41.580 --> 00:54:44.880
I thought was kind of cool because they make a bunch of different candies.
我觉得挺酷的，因为他们生产一大堆不同的糖果。

1232
00:54:45.360 --> 00:54:45.860
Mm-hmm.
嗯哼。

1233
00:54:45.860 --> 00:54:51.180
They make 800,000 sweet tart rolls every day.
他们每天生产80万卷甜酸卷糖。

1234
00:54:51.820 --> 00:54:56.640
It's the size of 2,700 football fields a day.
这相当于每天2700个足球场的大小。

1235
00:54:56.880 --> 00:54:57.560
Wow.
哇。

1236
00:54:57.880 --> 00:54:59.400
They run out of Chicago.
他们的总部在芝加哥。

1237
00:55:00.000 --> 00:55:02.100
They also make nerds.
他们还生产乐透糖（Nerds）。

1238
00:55:02.200 --> 00:55:02.560
Oh.
噢。

1239
00:55:02.740 --> 00:55:03.720
The candy nerds.
就是那个乐透糖果。

1240
00:55:03.900 --> 00:55:04.100
Yeah.
对。

1241
00:55:04.440 --> 00:55:09.300
And they make 135,000 pounds of nerds every day.
而且他们每天生产13.5万磅的乐透糖。

1242
00:55:09.940 --> 00:55:10.340
Whoa.
哇哦。

1243
